  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Hungarian magyar nóta (\"Hungarian song\") urban-popular song-form developing in nineteenth-century Hungarian cities as composed-popular-song with Hungarian-Gypsy-scale modal characteristics, performed by Roma cigányzenekar bands at coffee-houses, restaurants, and private parties. Distinguished from authentic peasant népdal folk-song (which Bartók and Kodály championed against magyar nóta as ethnographic-cultural-purity) by magyar nóta's composed-individual-author (vs anonymous-collective-folk) compositional framework. Performance: solo singer with cigányzenekar ensemble of first-violin (prímás), second-violin and viola accompaniment, cimbalom, clarinet, and double-bass. The genre remained dominant in twentieth-century Hungarian urban popular culture and continues today as a sentimental cultural form.",
